// Enforces `.claude/rules/money.md`: a monetary amount never becomes a `number`.
//
// This is the most load-bearing lint rule in the repo, and it lives in a plugin rather than
// in `biome.jsonc` because Biome ships no `no-restricted-syntax` equivalent -- GritQL
// plugins are the replacement for the ESLint rule this file was ported from.
//
// Why a machine has to hold this: the platform goes to real lengths to keep money out of a
// float -- `big.js` end to end, NUMERIC(18,4), Postgres type parsers pinned to return
// `numeric` as a string, wire types carrying the digits as a string, and a gate test that
// walks every schema looking for a bare number under a money-shaped key. All of it is undone
// by one `Number(invoice.total)`, because JavaScript's only numeric type IS a float. That
// mistake does not raise. It arrives in a dashboard looking exactly like a fact, and a
// reviewer cannot reliably catch it.
//
// `parseFloat` as a bare global is handled by `noRestrictedGlobals` in `biome.jsonc`; it is
// a name lookup, not a syntax shape, so the rule engine expresses it better than a pattern.
//
// A ban on `.toFixed()` was tried in the ESLint original and removed deliberately: a syntax
// pattern cannot tell `Number#toFixed` (rounds an already-lossy float) from `Big#toFixed`
// (exact, and the correct way to render) without type information, so it fired on every
// legitimate `toBig(m).plus(x).toFixed(4)`. A rule that fights correct code gets worked
// around, and then it protects nothing. The coercions banned below already stop a `number`
// from existing in a money path, and payload numbers are read through lossless-json rather
// than `JSON.parse`.

language js

pattern money_coercions() {
  or {
    // `Number(x)`. Matched as a call so `Number` as a type annotation, or as a namespace
    // (`Number.isInteger`), is untouched -- only the coercion is.
    `Number($_)` as $call where {
      register_diagnostic(span=$call, message="Number() produces a float. Money is a string end to end -- parse it with parseAmount() from @undercroft/core and format it with formatMoney().", severity="error")
    },
    `Number.parseFloat($_)` as $call where {
      register_diagnostic(span=$call, message="Number.parseFloat produces a float. See @undercroft/core/money.", severity="error")
    },
    // `Number.parseInt` is deliberately NOT banned here, though the ESLint rule this file
    // was ported from did ban it. Under ESLint the legal spelling for parsing an integer
    // was the bare global `parseInt`, and the codebase uses it for ports, key versions and
    // the sub-second digits of a stamp -- each with a comment saying "parseInt, not
    // Number(): a port, not an amount".
    //
    // Biome inverts that. `useNumberNamespace` (on under `preset: "all"`) rewrites bare
    // `parseInt` to `Number.parseInt`, so porting the old ban verbatim left NO legal way to
    // parse an integer and flagged 14 correct call sites. Banning both spellings of the
    // same function is not strictness, it is a contradiction, and `money.md` is explicit
    // that a rule which fights correct code gets worked around and then protects nothing.
    //
    // The intent the rule actually encodes survives intact: `Number()` and `parseFloat` are
    // banned because they produce a float from something that might be an amount. An
    // integer index is not an amount.
    // `Big#toNumber()` is the float you spent a library avoiding. It exists for charting,
    // and a chart is not a ledger.
    `$_.toNumber()` as $call where {
      register_diagnostic(span=$call, message="toNumber() discards precision. Keep the Big, or render with toFixed(); see @undercroft/core/money.", severity="error")
    },
    // Unary `+`. The literal exclusion is carried over from the ESLint selector
    // (`[argument.type!='Literal']`): `+1` is a number that was always a number, while
    // `+row.amount` is a coercion and the thing this ban exists for.
    `+$operand` as $coercion where {
      // Non-capturing groups are required: GritQL reads a regex capture group as a variable
      // binding and refuses the pattern ("matched 1 variables, but expected 0").
      $operand <: not r"(?:[0-9][0-9._e]*|\"[^\"]*\"|'[^']*')",
      register_diagnostic(span=$coercion, message="Unary + coerces to a float. If this is money, use formatMoney(); if it is a count, it is already a number.", severity="error")
    }
  }
}

money_coercions()
